1. `GetFileSize()` Function: 

   The `GetFileSize()` function is not a built-in function, so you'll need to implement it yourself to obtain the file size before reading the contents of the included file.

   One implementation could be to use the `stat()` function in C to obtain the size of the file. Here's how you can add this function to the `glslx_preprocessor.c` file:

   ```
   #include <sys/stat.h>

   int GetFileSize(char *filename) {
       struct stat file_stats;

       if (stat(filename, &file_stats) == 0) {
           return (int) file_stats.st_size;
       } else {
           return -1; // Handle file not found error or permission denied error
       }
   }
   ```

   This implementation uses the `stat()` function to return the size of the file by querying its `st_size` field. If an error occurs, it returns -1 for you to handle accordingly.

2. Different GLSL Versions:

   To configure your preprocessor to recognize different types of GLSL versions, such as GLSL-S and GLSL-E, you can add flags to your main routine to indicate which version of GLSL you're targeting. Then, you can use conditional compilation to adapt certain preprocessing behaviors based on the GLSL version.

   For example, here's how you could add a new argument to your main function to indicate which GLSL version is being targeted:

   ```
   int main(int argc, char **argv) {
       ...

       if (argc < 3) {
           printf("Usage: %s FILE_NAME [INCLUDE_PATHS...] [GLSL_TYPE]\n", argv[0]);
           return 1;
       }

       char *filename = argv[1];
       char *include_paths_str = argv[2];
       char *glsl_type = argc >= 4 ? argv[3] : "GLSL";

       int include_path_count = argc - 2;

       char **include_paths = (char **) malloc((include_path_count + 1) * sizeof(char *));
       for (int I = 2; I <= include_path_count + 1; i++) {
           include_paths[i - 2] = strdup(argv[i]);
       }
       context.include_path = include_paths;
       context.include_path_count = include_path_count;

       glslx_process_file(&context, filename, include_paths_str, glsl_type);

       for (int I = 0; I < include_path_count; i++) {
           free(include_paths[i]);
       }
       free(context.output_buf);
       free(include_paths);

       return 0;
   }
   ```

   Here, we added a new argument called `GLSL_TYPE` to indicate which version of GLSL is being targeted. If not provided, it defaults to "GLSL" (i.e., generic GLSL).

   You can then use conditional compilation to adapt inclusion preprocessing behavior based on the GLSL version. For example, if GLSL-S, you may want to ignore the `#version` directive and include a special header file.

   Here's an example of how you could make changes based on GLSL version:

   ```
   char *glslx_process_file(glslx_include_context_t *context, char *filename, char *include_paths_str, char *glsl_type) {
       char *content = NULL;
       FILE *input_file = fopen(filename, "r");

       if (input_file) {
           content = (char *) malloc(buffer_size);

           int read_count;
           while ((read_count = fread(content + context->output_buf_pos, 1, buffer_size - (context->output_buf_pos % buffer_size), input_file))) {
               context->output_buf_pos += read_count;
               if (context->output_buf_pos > context->output_buf_size) {
                   context->output_buf_size = context->output_buf_pos + buffer_size;

                   char *new_buf = (char *) realloc(context->output_buf, context->output_buf_size);
                   if (!new_buf) {
                       // Handle out of memory error
                   }

                   context->output_buf = new_buf;
               }

               char *line = strtok((char *) (content + context->output_buf_pos - read_count), "\n");
               while (line) {
                   if (glsl_type == "GLSL-S") { // Ignore #version directive: assume it is set externally
                       char *ptr = strtok(line, " \n");
                       if (strncmp(ptr, "#version", 8) == 0) {
                           line += strlen(ptr) + 1; // Skip "#version" followed by a space
                       }
                   }

                   glslx_process_directive(context, line);
                   line = strtok(NULL, "\n");
               }

               fseek(input_file, -read_count, SEEK_CUR);
               context->output_buf_pos -= read_count;
           }

           fclose(input_file);
       } else {
           // Handle file not found error
       }

       return context->output_buf;
   }

   void glslx_process_directive(glslx_include_context_t *context, char *line) {
       if (strncmp(line, "#include ", 9) == 0) {
           char *include_filename = strchr(line + 9, '"') + 1;
           char *ptr = strchr(include_filename, '"');
           *ptr = '\0';

           int include_length = glslx_process_file_inclusion(context, include_filename);

           if (include_length > 0) {
               // Adapt the logic to include files based on GLSL version here

               // For example, GLSL-S could include a special header file:
               // strcat(strncat(new_output_buf, "#define GLSL_S 1\n", context->output_buf_pos), include_filename);
               // strcat(new_output_buf + context->output_buf_pos + strlen(context->output_buf) + strlen(include_filename) + 1, "\n");
           } else {
               fprintf(stderr, "ERROR: Could not find file '%s' in include paths.\n", include_filename);
           }
       }
   }
   ```

   Here, we added conditional compilation based on the `glsl_type` argument to ignore the `#version` directive in GLSL-S and to include a special header file. You'll need to modify accordingly to support any other GLSL version, 

   Again, this is just an example, and you'll need to adjust the behavior of the preprocessor accordingly for other features or limitations of GLSL-S or GLSL-E.

Una manera de crear un preprocesador GLSLX para agregar compatibilidad con directivas `#include` podría ser la siguiente aproximación:

1. Crear un script en un lenguaje de programación de tu elección (como Python, Node.js, o C++) que lee los archivos GLSLX con las directivas `#include`.
2. Al encontrar una directiva `#include`, realice la siguiente transformación: 
   - Leia el contenido del archivo GLSLX en el que se encuentre la directiva `#include`.
   - Copie todo el contenido del archivo incluido en el lugar de la directiva `#include` original.
   - Elimine la directiva de inclusión.
   - Ejecute el proceso de compilación en el texto resultante concatenado.
3. Utiliza un módulo de compilación GLSL para compilar el código que resultará de las transformaciones anteriores.

Para preparar los archivos GLSLX, puedes definir una sintaxis de convenciones personalizadas para las directivas `#include`. Por ejemplo:

```glsl
// Archivo principal (archivo.glslx)

precision highp float;
#include <file.glslx> // Incluye el contenido de file.glslx en este lugar
uniform vec4 myUniform;

void main() {
  // ...
}
```

En `file.glslx`, puedes definir código GLSL que incluir:

```glsl
// Archivo a incluir (file.glslx)

float myFunction(float x) {
  // ...
}

vec4 computeResult(vec4 input) {
  // ...
}
```

Al compilar `archivo.glslx`, el preprocesador encontrará la directiva `#include`, leerá el contenido de `file.glslx`, copiara el contenido de `file.glslx` en el lugar de la directiva de inclusión, y compilará el texto resultante con una biblioteca de compilación GLSL.

Este enfoque permitirá incluir múltiples archivos con la sintaxis adecuada, creando un sistema de modulares y reutilizables. Sin embargo, es importante tener en cuenta que las directivas `#include` no son parte de la especificación oficial de GLSL. Por lo tanto, tu implementación puede no ser portable entre todas las plataformas y compiladores GLSL.

