Sure, here are some additional examples of how desstructuring can be used in React components:

1. Nested object properties

Suppose you have an object with nested properties and want to extract them as separate variables. You can use the same syntax, but with dot notation:

```
const MyComponent = ({ user }) => {
  const { firstName, lastName, address: { city, country } } = user;

  return (
    <div>
      {firstName} {lastName}, {city}, {country}
    </div>
  );
}
```

2. Array values

If you have an array of values as part of your props and you want to loop through them, you can desstructure the array and its values:

```
const MyComponent = ({ items }) => {
  const [itemOne, itemTwo, itemThree] = items;

  return (
    <ul>
      <li>{itemOne}</li>
      <li>{itemTwo}</li>
      <li>{itemThree}</li>
    </ul>
  );
}
```

3. Rest props

If you have additional props that you want to manage separately, you can use rest destructuring to group them:

```
const MyComponent = ({ name, age, ...otherProps }) => {
  // do something with otherProps

  return (
    <div>
      My name is {name} and I am {age} years old
    </div>
  );
}
```

4. Using shorthand for object property assignment

When you have a simple one-to-one mapping between your props and variables, you can make use of shorthand notation:

```
const MyComponent = ({ title, description }) => {
  const { title: pageTitle, description: pageDescription } = props;

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{pageTitle}</h1>
      <p>{pageDescription}</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

5. Mix object and array destructuring

When you want to destructure both objects and arrays in a single line, you can use a mix of both syntax:

```
const MyComponent = ({ items, settings }) => {
  const { items: [itemOne, itemTwo], boolSetting, stringSetting } = settings;

  return (
    <div>
      {itemOne} and {itemTwo}
      <br />
      boolSetting is {boolSetting}
      <br />
      stringSetting is {stringSetting}
    </div>
  );
}
```

